Design notes:
    For the data structure of the dictionary, we chose an array of structs that contains two string pointers: one to the original-case spelling and one to the lowercase version. We used binary search on the lowercase words (sorted using qsort) to check for spelling errors and then compare it to original if found. The words loaded from the dictionary and temporary copies of the file paths are both dynamically allocated. Additionally, we implemented the buffer strategy to scan words from the input text files to reduce system calls.



Test Plan:
    Explicit-file scan
    Command: ./spell dict2 [file1.txt] [file2.txt]test_dir2/file3.txt
    What it tests: that the program scans only the explicitly listed files, reports misspellings in the format filename:line:col word (to stderr), and returns non-zero when any misspelling is found.

    Recursive directory scan (default .txt)
    Command: ./spell dict2 test_dir2
    What it tests: recursive traversal into subdirectories and scanning of all files ending with the default .txt suffix; verifies correct filename/line/column reporting for each detected misspelling.

    Custom-suffix scan (.bar) with mixed explicit files and directories
    Command: ./spell -s .bar dict2 [file1.txt]test_dir2/sub_dir2 test_dir2/sub_dir2/sub_dir4
    What it tests: that -s <suffix> overrides the default suffix (the suffix must include a dot), explicit files passed on the command line are scanned regardless of suffix, and that directories are searched recursively for files matching the custom suffix.
    Example: using dictionary `dict2` and reading files in the directories `test_dir2/sub_dir1` and `test_dir2/sub_dir2` (recursively)

    Error and argument validation checks
    Commands: e.g., ./spell nonexist, missing args, or malformed -s usage
    What it tests: proper error messages for invalid or missing dictionary files, missing arguments, and incorrect -s usage.

    Capitalization and near-match detection
    Setup: dict2 contains mixed-case entries (e.g., KiWI, Cherry) and test files include capitalization variants and close misspellings (e.g., applle, bannnana).
    What it tests: case-handling rules (lowercasing for lookup plus a capitalization check) and correct identification of true misspellings versus acceptable capitalized forms.

    Tokenization and punctuation handling
    What it tests: stripping leading opening punctuation (e.g., ([{'"), trimming trailing non-alphanumeric characters, ignoring tokens with no letters (numbers/symbol-only), and handling hyphens/apostrophes according to the scanner logic.

    Overlapping directories (duplication observation)
    Example: passing both a parent directory and its nested subdirectory in one invocation.
    What it tests / notes: files in the nested directory will be visited more than once (once via recursion and again via the explicit nested argument), producing duplicate reports; deduplication is not implemented by default.

    Use these runs to reproduce and verify the program’s argument parsing, traversal behavior, suffix filtering, tokenization, capitalization rules, reporting format, and exit-code behavior.